    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the smallest positive integer \\( b \\) such that the quadratic expression \\( x^2 + bx + 2008 \\) can be factored into a product of two binomials with integer coefficients, we need to express it in the form \\( (x + p)(x + q) \\). Here, \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are integers, and we have the relationship:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + bx + 2008 = (x + p)(x + q)\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side gives:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + bx + 2008 = x^2 + (p+q)x + pq\n\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients from both sides, we find that:\n\n\\[\nb = p + q \\quad \\text{and} \\quad pq = 2008\n\\]\n\nTo find the smallest positive \\( b \\), we need to factor \\( 2008 \\) into two positive integers and then find the sum of these factors. Let's start by finding the factor pairs of \\( 2008 \\):\n\n\\[\n2008 = 2^3 \\times 251\n\\]\n\nWe can list the factor pairs:\n\n\\[\n(1, 2008), (2, 1004), (4, 502), (8, 251)\n\\]\n\nWe need to check each pair to see which one gives the smallest sum:\n\n1. For the pair \\( (1, 2008) \\):\n   \\[\n   p + q = 1 + 2008 = 2009\n   \\]\n\n2. For the pair \\( (2, 1004) \\):\n   \\[\n   p + q = 2 + 1004 = 1006\n   \\]\n\n3. For the pair \\( (4, 502) \\):\n   \\[\n   p + q = 4 + 502 = 506\n   \\]\n\n4. For the pair \\( (8, 251) \\):\n   \\[\n   p + q = 8 + 251 = 259\n   \\]\n\nAmong these sums, the smallest is \\( 2009 \\). Therefore, the smallest positive integer \\( b \\) for which \\( x^2 + bx + 2008 \\) can be factored into a product of two binomials with integer coefficients is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2009}\n\\]",
